"""plain_text.py - plain, student-level English for the report.

Keys are positions in the report's content list (see build_report.py). Citation markers [@key] and
cross-references {F:key} / {T:key} must be kept exactly as they are; build_report.py turns them into numbers.
PLAIN / LISTS replace text in the full report. SHORT / SHORT_LISTS replace it again in the short report
(None = leave this paragraph out).
"""

PLAIN = {
    2: "Tourism is one of the biggest sources of income and jobs in Uganda. In 2024 the country received 1,371,895 international visitors, which is 7.7% more than the 1,274,210 in 2023, and tourism earned a record USD 1.28 billion. This was about 16% of all the country's exports [@mtwa2024]. The Uganda Tourism Satellite Account for 2024 puts the direct contribution of tourism at UGX 6.99 trillion, about 3.3% of the economy [@tsa2024]. Tourism is also the fifth largest employer in Uganda, with 876,512 direct jobs [@wb_jobs]. Domestic tourism is growing too: more than 3.2 million Ugandans travelled within the country in 2024 [@tsa2024].",
    3: "Most of Uganda's tourism depends on nature: national parks, wildlife and primates. The Tourism Development Master Plan 2014-2024 aimed to develop these products and to grow domestic tourism, with a target of more than USD 1.4 billion in yearly earnings [@masterplan]. Conservation education centres are part of this. The Uganda Wildlife Conservation Education Centre (UWEC) in Entebbe is a zoo and education centre where visitors see many of Uganda's animals up close. It reported more than 483,000 visitors in 2024, mostly Ugandans [@uwec_visitors].",
    5: "A wildlife visit is only as good as the explanation that comes with it. Visitors want to know what animal they are looking at, how it lives, how to stay safe around it and what it is called. In Uganda this mostly depends on human guides, and several problems have been reported:",
    8: "Smart tourism means using information technology, and more and more artificial intelligence (AI), to improve the visitor experience and to help destinations manage visitors [@gretzel]. Reviews of AI in tourism describe uses such as recommendations, forecasting, chatbots and visitor services [@tussyadiah,@samara], and mobile apps are the main way tourists use these services on site [@dorcic]. For wildlife tourism, the most direct use of AI is naming a species from a photo, which has mainly been studied for scientific camera-trap monitoring [@tuia,@norouzzadeh]. Deep convolutional neural networks (CNNs) can now identify animals in millions of camera-trap images [@norouzzadeh,@tabak,@willi], give results that ecologists can use without checking every image by hand [@whytock], and are available in platforms and in two-step pipelines that first detect an animal and then classify it [@velez,@beery19,@pwild].",
    9: "On a visitor's phone, the same technology can work as a digital guide that is always available. The visitor photographs an animal and the app gives its name, short facts, a safety note and its name in local languages. If the model runs on the phone itself, the app also works without a network. The app does not replace guides. It helps them, it serves visitors who have no guide, and it can give the same checked information in local languages to everyone.",
    10: "This report is about Part 2 of the UgaWildlifeVision project, where we built such a tool called UgaWildVision. In Part 1 we corrected the NESTLER Wild Animal Recognition Video Dataset (its Safari part, filmed in Ugandan parks) [@nestler] into a seven-class dataset and trained a YOLOv8-nano detector, and we published the dataset, software and report on Zenodo [@z_data,@z_soft,@z_report]. In Part 2 the task changes from drawing boxes in park videos to naming the species in a visitor's phone photo, and the result is an offline app that runs fully on the phone.",
    13: "The biggest known weakness of wildlife classifiers is that they do not work well in new conditions. Beery et al. showed that models do very well at the camera locations they were trained on, but badly at new locations, especially classifiers [@beery18]. Schneider et al. found that accuracy dropped for species photographed at locations not seen in training, and listed the size of the training set, class imbalance and location as important factors [@schneider]. Norman et al. tested whether CNN species classification works across different habitats in one survey [@norman]. The WILDS benchmark showed the same problem in many areas, including camera traps: performance on new data is much lower than on data like the training set, and the usual fixes do not close the gap [@wilds]. A tourist's phone photo is new data for a model trained on camera-trap and internet images. This is why we kept a test set made of our own phone photos, which the model never trains on.",
    15: "When local labelled data are scarce, many projects borrow images from public sources. Shepley et al. trained detectors on Flickr and iNaturalist photos, and these worked better on new camera-trap sites than models trained on camera-trap images alone. Adding 5-10% images from the target site helped even more [@shepley]. Large citizen-science collections such as iNat2021 [@inat21] and the TreeOfLife-10M data used for the BioCLIP model [@bioclip] show how far this idea can go. Gadot et al. found that classifying cropped animals instead of whole images improved the macro F1 score by about 25% on a large camera-trap dataset [@gadot]. This matters here because we use crops from the NESTLER Safari dataset together with whole photos.",
    17: "Models that run on a phone must be small. MobileNetV3 was designed for mobile processors [@mobilenet], and tests on Android phones show big differences in speed between phone chips [@aibench]. The Ultralytics YOLOv8 family includes small image classifiers (YOLOv8n-cls and YOLOv8s-cls) that are pretrained on ImageNet [@yolov8]. A trained model can be exported to the ONNX format and run in a web browser with ONNX Runtime Web, which uses WebAssembly (WASM) [@ort]. Progressive web apps (PWAs) can be installed on the home screen and work offline from one web code base, so they have been suggested as a way to combine web and native app development [@pwa]. For tourists this means there is no app store download and no network is needed once the app is installed.",
    19: "Data augmentation is the usual first step when data are limited [@shorten]. RandAugment cuts the search for augmentation settings down to two parameters [@randaug] and is the default in Ultralytics classification training. An app for tourists also needs to know when it is not sure. Modern networks are often over-confident [@guo], and their confidence becomes even less reliable when the data change [@ovadia], although the highest softmax probability is still a useful simple signal for rejecting doubtful inputs [@hendrycks]. Another problem is data leakage between training and test sets, which is a common reason for results that look too good in machine-learning research [@kapoor]. We met this in Part 1, where a time-ordered split hid one class. We train with the AdamW optimiser [@adamw] and an ImageNet-pretrained backbone.",
    21: "Tourism earns Uganda more than USD 1.28 billion a year and depends largely on wildlife [@mtwa2024,@masterplan]. Yet what a visitor learns about the wildlife depends on guides who are uneven in skill, rarely speak local languages and are missing for visitors who come alone [@wb_jobs,@dispatch], at places where we cannot assume network access [@datareportal]. We found no free tool that lets a visitor photograph an animal at a Ugandan wildlife site and get, offline, its name, short facts, a safety note and its names in local languages. Building one with AI also has a technical problem. Our labelled images come from three places: the NESTLER Safari video dataset, internet image sources and our own 47 photos taken at UWEC in Entebbe (Section 2.2). All of them help the model, but together they are still small and not varied enough, and we had no time to draw bounding boxes. So the question is how well a small classifier trained on these images can identify animals in real visitors' phone photos at a place like UWEC, and what limits it most.",
    23: "The main objective is to build and test an AI-based phone tool that names Ugandan wildlife species from a visitor's photo, to improve the wildlife tourism experience in Uganda. The specific objectives are to:",
    30: "Section 2 explains the methodology and Section 3 the experimental setup. Section 4 gives the results and Section 5 analyses them with graphs. Section 6 discusses the results, including what they mean for tourism. Section 7 lists the limitations and problems we met, and Section 8 gives the conclusion and future work.",
    33: "Our approach has five stages ({F:pipeline}): (1) collect the images and record the source and correct label of each one; (2) build a classification dataset and split it carefully; (3) fine-tune pretrained YOLOv8 classifiers with several random seeds; (4) choose a model using validation accuracy and test it on two test sets; and (5) export the model and run it inside a progressive web app. We chose image classification instead of detection because it only needs a folder of photos for each species and no boxes. The disadvantage is that a classifier names only one animal per photo and works best when the animal fills much of the picture.",
    36: "We used three sources of images, and all three contribute to the model. The first is the NESTLER Safari video dataset, which is part of the NESTLER Wild Animal Recognition Video Dataset on Zenodo [@nestler,@z_data]. It has video frames from Kibale, Queen Elizabeth and Bwindi Impenetrable National Parks (February to June 2025): 1,220 frames and 3,909 boxes in seven classes. We cut each box out as a crop with 15% extra space around it and skipped boxes shorter than 32 pixels. The second source is internet images (images.cv, iNaturalist and other websites), and the third is our own 47 phone photos taken at UWEC in Entebbe. The internet images and our own photos reached us as three zip files of 5,187 images in folders by animal; one zip was a copy of another, so we did not use it twice. A script (build_manifest.py) recorded the size, camera data (EXIF), hash, folder, corrected class and source of every image, and the file image_sources.csv lists where each internet image came from. {T:sources} lists the sources.",
    38: "UWEC is in Entebbe, so our own photos and the UWEC dataset come from the same place. All three sources help the model. The NESTLER and internet images are used for training, validation and testing. Our own 47 photos are used as the final real-world test, because they look most like what a visitor takes. We kept them out of training on purpose, so that at least one test is completely independent of the training data. The internet images are only used inside this project and are not shared again, in the app or in this report, and no figure in the report shows an internet photo. We did not use the species as evidence of where an image came from, because UWEC also keeps animals from other continents, such as tigers.",
    40: "Folder names were not always correct. We looked at pictures from every folder and fixed the labels listed in {T:labels}. For example, some pictures in the buffalo folder were zebras. Two Samsung photos were stored sideways, with the rotation saved in their EXIF data, so every script turns them upright first.",
    43: "The dataset has 26 classes. Twenty regular classes have at least about 30 images each: uganda_kob, bird, chimpanzee, elephant, gorilla, hippo and warthog (mostly from the NESTLER crops), and tiger, leopard, fox, frog, snake, fish, zebra, monkey, crocodile, tortoise, giraffe, rhino and squirrel. At the team's request we also kept five classes with very few images and marked them in the app: lion (6 training images), buffalo (1), cheetah (4), kudu (14) and hyena (10). An 'other' class holds about 25 species with 4 to 10 images each (for example kangaroo, jackal, gazelle and impala), so that the app can say 'not one of my species' instead of forcing a guess.",
    45: "We split the data by recording session or photo group, never by single images, because nearly identical images on both sides of a split make the test score look better than it really is [@kapoor]. {T:splitrules} shows the rules, and {F:splitcounts} in Section 5 shows the number of images per class.",
    48: "The classifier is YOLOv8n-cls from Ultralytics [@yolov8]. It has a convolutional backbone pretrained on ImageNet, followed by a classification head that turns the features it found into one score z_k for each class. The backbone is the part that finds patterns in the image. With 26 classes it has 1,471,594 parameters and needs 3.4 GFLOPs for one 224 x 224 image. We also trained the larger YOLOv8s-cls (5,114,042 parameters, 12.6 GFLOPs) once for comparison. The softmax function turns the scores into probabilities, and training reduces the cross-entropy loss, which measures how far the predicted probabilities are from the true class y:",
    51: "Fine-tuning starts from the pretrained weights and updates all layers. We use the AdamW optimiser [@adamw] with a cosine learning-rate schedule. During training Ultralytics also applies RandAugment [@randaug], random horizontal flips, colour changes and random erasing to every training image.",
    53: "In a second experiment (v2) we asked whether making the training images look more like phone photos would reduce the gap to our UWEC photos. We fixed the recipe before seeing any v2 result, and every step has the same probability for every class, so it cannot become a shortcut for one class. For each training image we added K = 2 changed copies:",
    55: "The validation, test and UWEC images are the same in v1 and v2; only the training images change. One thing to admit: the fence idea came from looking at our UWEC photos, which are the test images.",
    57: "We choose the model for the app using the best validation top-1 accuracy, never the test or UWEC accuracy. The validation set (553 images) is used to pick the best epoch (one full pass through the training images) and the best run, and to tell training when to stop. We report two test sets: the test set (559 images from the same sources as the training data) and uwec_test (our 47 phone photos). The metrics are:",
    61: "Top-1 is the share of photos named correctly. Top-5 is the share where the true class is among the five most likely answers. Macro accuracy is the average of the accuracy of each class, so large classes do not hide the small ones. For the app's 'not sure' rule, coverage is the share of photos whose highest probability is at least the threshold, and accuracy when answered is the top-1 accuracy on only those photos [@hendrycks]. We give results over seeds as mean ± standard deviation. To compare v1 with v2, we also match each UWEC image across the same seeds and count how many went from wrong to right and from right to wrong.",
    63: "We export the PyTorch model to ONNX [@ort]. The app has to prepare each photo exactly as training did, otherwise its answers will differ. Ultralytics uses torchvision Resize(224) (shorter side to 224 pixels), then CenterCrop(224) and scaling to the range 0 to 1. A check script (check_preprocess.py) compared four ways on every validation and test image: Ultralytics predict, torchvision, our copy of the app's resize and crop, and ONNX Runtime.",
    65: "UgaWildVision is a static PWA ({F:pwa}). The user takes a photo or picks one from the gallery. The app turns the photo upright, resizes and crops it the same way as in training, and runs the ONNX model with ONNX Runtime Web 1.31.1 (WASM, one thread, included in the app files instead of loaded from a CDN). It shows the top three species with their confidence. If the top confidence is below 0.7 it says it is not sure and asks for a closer, clearer photo. Each species page shows facts, a safety note for dangerous animals and a warning for the five classes with very few training images. The species file has places for names in Luganda, Runyankole, Rutooro and Ateso, as text and audio, which we generate with the Sunbird AI translation and text-to-speech service [@sunbird]. The app only shows names that pass our automatic check, and a native speaker still has to confirm them. A service worker saves all the files so the app works offline, and photos never leave the phone. The app is published over HTTPS on GitHub Pages, which phones need for camera access and offline use. At a site, a visitor can open the app from a link or QR code at the entrance, install it in seconds and use it anywhere in the site without a network.",
    67: "Around the classifier the app is built as a pocket field guide for visitors ({F:appscreens}). The Animals tab shows each species as a photo card, with search and filters for mammals, birds, reptiles, amphibians and fish. Every animal has its own page with a large photo, its name in the chosen language with a Listen button, tags for its IUCN conservation status and danger level, a taxonomy table (Latin and Swahili names, class, order and family) and short facts, for example about the tree-climbing lions of Ishasha or the return of rhinos to Ziwa Rhino Sanctuary. A camera button in the tab bar opens identification, and an 'I saw it!' button saves sightings on the phone, which the Sightings tab shows with a count of animals seen. The guide photos are openly licensed Wikimedia Commons pictures (CC0, public domain, CC BY or CC BY-SA only) with credit on each photo and in the app. Taxonomy and IUCN status come from Wikidata. No training image is shown in the app.",
    69: "The published site also has an install page (install.html) with a QR code ({F:qr}) and short instructions for Android and iPhone. It can be printed and put up at a site entrance or shared by guides, and the app shows the same code so one visitor can share it with another.",
    72: "Most UWEC visitors are Ugandans [@uwec_visitors] and guides often do not know local animal names [@dispatch], so the app gives each animal's name in four Ugandan languages: Luganda, Runyankole, Rutooro and Ateso, as text and, where possible, as audio. We generated the names with the Sunbird AI API [@sunbird] using a script (sunbird_languages.py). For each species and language it does four steps:",
    74: "All names are stored as not yet verified. A native speaker still has to confirm them. A play button speaks each name. The API keys are read from the environment and are never stored in files.",
    76: "Ugandan languages serve Ugandan visitors, but international visitors need their own languages. To avoid choosing them just by preference, we used a fixed rule on the latest official table of visitors by country of residence, from the Uganda Tourism Satellite Account (2019 survey, 1,542,620 visitors, of whom 297,726 came for leisure and holidays) [@tsa2023]. A language is included if visitors from countries where it is a main language make up at least 2% of all visitors or at least 2% of leisure and holiday visitors. English is always included. {T:langsel} and {F:langsel} show the result. Kiswahili (Kenya and Tanzania), French (DR Congo, Burundi, France and Belgium), German (Germany and Austria) and Dutch (the Netherlands) pass. Italian (1.98% of leisure visitors), Spanish, Arabic and Russian do not, and Russia is not even listed separately. These languages can be added if new statistics show more visitors from those countries.",
    79: "The Kiswahili names and audio come from Sunbird AI in the same way as the Ugandan languages. The French, German and Dutch names are everyday common names, checked against the titles of the matching Wikipedia articles in each language [@wikipedia]. Sunbird does not cover these languages. Their audio uses the phone's own voice through the Web Speech API [@webspeech], so no audio files are needed.",
    84: "All settings were fixed in train_cls.py so that every run can be compared. {T:train} lists the ones used for classification training, copied from the saved args.yaml of each run.",
    90: "The four v1 runs took 6 to 8 minutes each (17:06 to 17:34 on 22 November 2026). Because v2 has three times as many training images, each v2 epoch has three times as many steps. We noted this effect before running v2.",
    93: "All runs finished without errors and stopped early. Validation top-1 accuracy rose above 0.8 within a few epochs and stayed near 0.90 ({F:trainval}). Training and validation loss are in {F:loss}. The best validation scores were 0.901, 0.899 and 0.910 for the three YOLOv8n-cls seeds and 0.911 for YOLOv8s-cls. By our rule, v1_n_seed2 (0.910, the best small model) was chosen for the app.",
    98: "On the test set, which comes from the same sources as the training data, the classifier names the species correctly about 91% of the time, and the true species is almost always in the top five. On our own UWEC phone photos it is right about 19% of the time (1 photo in 5), and the true species is in the top five less than half the time ({T:summary}). Test macro accuracy (0.73 to 0.81) is lower than top-1 because some small classes do badly.",
    100: "On the test set, 11 classes were classified perfectly by v1_n_seed2, while uganda_kob (44/60), bird (48/60) and gorilla (26/30) were the weakest of the larger classes ({F:pctest}). On our UWEC photos, only zebra and tiger worked well ({T:uwecclass}, {F:pcuwec}). Chimpanzee, bird and buffalo were never correct in any run. So the model does well in some situations and needs more varied photos in others.",
    105: "{F:cmtest} and {F:cmuwec} show the confusion matrices of v1_n_seed2. On the test set almost all answers are on the diagonal (correct). On the UWEC photos most are off the diagonal (wrong).",
    110: "At the app's threshold of 0.7, the model answers 94% of test photos with 94% accuracy. On UWEC photos it still answers 77% of the photos, but only 22% of those answers are right. Even above 0.9 it answers 43% of photos with only 25% accuracy ({T:thresh}).",
    113: "Phone-style augmentation raised the standard test accuracy by about 0.015 but did not improve accuracy on the UWEC photos ({T:v1v2}). By our selection rule the app keeps v1_n_seed2.",
    117: "Sunbird AI produced 125 names (25 species in 5 languages). Of these, 44 passed the back-translation check and are shown in the app, and 39 of them have audio ({T:langnames}, {F:langcheck}). Common animals with a well-known local word were translated well, for example lion (Luganda Empologoma, Runyankole Entare) and bird (Ekinyonyi). Animals with compound or foreign names, such as Uganda kob, chimpanzee or kudu, often came back as unrelated sentences or as the English word.",
    120: "{T:visitornames} lists the French, German and Dutch names used for international visitors.",
    124: "{F:testuwec} shows the two test sets side by side for each v1 run. All four runs score 0.90 to 0.92 on the test set and 0.15 to 0.23 on the UWEC photos. The gap is about 0.7 for every seed and both model sizes, so it comes from the data and not from one unlucky run. {F:runmetrics} adds top-5 and macro accuracy. On the test set top-5 is almost 1.0, but on UWEC photos even the five best guesses contain the right species less than half the time, so the errors are not near misses.",
    128: "{F:sources} shows where the images come from. Most training images are NESTLER Safari crops and internet images, while our UWEC test photos are a kind of image the model never saw in training. {F:splitcounts} shows the images per class. The classes with very few images have more UWEC test photos than training images (buffalo has 1 training image and 9 test photos; lion has 6 against 9), so we could not expect them to work.",
    132: "{T:errors} lists what v1_n_seed2 predicted for each UWEC class. The mistakes follow the training data. Chimpanzee has 400 training images, but almost all come from one NESTLER video, so a chimpanzee photographed through a fence is called a monkey or a gorilla. The NESTLER birds are small egrets in video frames, while the UWEC birds are an owl, parrots and ostriches, which the model calls frog or fish. Buffalo, with one training image, becomes elephant.",
    135: "{F:conf} compares the confidence of correct and wrong answers. On the test set wrong answers are rare and mostly less confident, so a threshold separates them well. On UWEC photos 37 of 47 answers are wrong and their average confidence is 0.80, so most of them are above the 0.7 threshold. {F:thresh} shows the same thing as curves: a higher threshold steadily improves test accuracy, but on UWEC photos it only removes answers without making the remaining ones more accurate.",
    139: "{F:v1v2} summarises the v2 experiment with the spread between seeds. The test score goes up a little, but the UWEC scores overlap within their spread. Per class ({F:v1v2class}), chimpanzee, bird and buffalo stay at zero, cheetah improves, and zebra, tiger and crocodile go down. With only 1 to 9 photos per class, none of these changes is real evidence. The validation curves ({F:v1v2val}) show that v2 did not reach a better validation score either.",
    144: "YOLOv8s-cls has 3.5 times the parameters and 3.7 times the computation of YOLOv8n-cls, but its validation top-1 is only 0.001 higher than the best small model (0.911 against 0.910), and its UWEC accuracy (0.234) is inside the range of the small-model seeds ({T:summary}). For a phone app the small model is the better choice.",
    147: "The main finding is a gap of about 0.7 between accuracy on the standard test set (0.909 ± 0.007) and accuracy on real phone photos (0.191 ± 0.030). This agrees with camera-trap research, where models that do very well at familiar locations fail at new ones [@beery18,@schneider,@wilds], and with Part 1 of this project, where gorilla detection dropped from 0.77 to 0.15 mAP50 on a session the model had not seen. The standard test set shares its sources and image style with the training set, while our UWEC photos do not. Only the second tells us what a user of the app will really get.",
    149: "The model does well on animals whose training images are many different photos of many different animals. Zebra and tiger come from varied internet photos and were recognised at UWEC in almost every run. It does badly on animals whose training images, however many, look alike: 400 chimpanzee crops from essentially one video, and NESTLER birds that are a different kind of bird at a different size. This matches the finding that the number of different scenes matters more than the number of images [@schneider], and that a few images from the target place can help a model trained on public photos [@shepley].",
    151: "Phone-style augmentation changes how an image looks: compression, fences, blur and a smaller animal. It cannot create what is missing, such as a chimpanzee from a new angle in a new place, or an owl when the training birds are egrets. We expected this before running v2, and the result agrees: classes at zero stayed at zero. The small rise on the standard test is the kind of 'better on the easy test' result that we should not trust [@kapoor]. We also did not tune the recipe on the UWEC photos, because with 47 images that would only fit the test set.",
    153: "The 0.7 threshold works as intended on the test set but not on phone photos, where the model is wrong even when it is very confident. This is the over-confidence described when data change [@guo,@ovadia]. A softmax probability is a useful signal for rejecting doubtful inputs [@hendrycks] only when the inputs look like the training data. So the app's start screen says that accuracy on real phone photos is about 1 in 5 and asks for close, clear photos.",
    155: "The engineering side worked. The ONNX model is 5.9 MB, takes about 8 ms per image on a laptop CPU and about 40 to 50 ms in a browser, and gives exactly the same answer as PyTorch on all 606 checked images once we matched the preprocessing. The PWA installs, works offline and keeps photos on the phone. This fits what is known about small CNNs and web delivery on phones [@mobilenet,@aibench,@pwa]. The limit is the data, not the model size or the delivery platform.",
    157: "Published wildlife classifiers report 88% to 99% accuracy inside their own datasets [@norouzzadeh,@willi] and lower values at new locations, for example 68.7% top-1 for the best network on locations it had not seen in [@schneider]. Those systems train on hundreds of thousands to millions of images from the target cameras. Our project has 3,636 training images and almost none from the target setting, so 0.19 on phone photos is not surprising. The lesson from [@shepley] and from our work is the same: a modest number of real photos of each species from the target place is likely to help more than any further training trick.",
    159: "For tourism the results say two things. First, the delivery side of an AI visitor tool is ready now. A 5.9 MB model runs offline on the phone in well under a second, needs no app store or network, keeps visitors' photos private and can carry facts, safety notes and local-language names for every species. This deals with the access problems in Section 1.2: limited internet [@datareportal], visitors without a guide and few local-language names [@dispatch].",
    160: "Second, recognition on real visitor photos is not yet good enough to rely on: about 1 photo in 5 at UWEC, with chimpanzee and birds, two of the animals visitors most want to see, never correct. At the same time the app already does well in other cases, such as zebra and tiger and the standard test. Releasing it to tourists in its current state could give wrong information and lose their trust. The cause is clear and can be fixed: the model has seen very few photos taken the way visitors take them. Tourism sites see the same animals every day, so they are well placed to collect exactly the missing photos. Guides and staff could photograph each animal over a few weeks and give the 30 or more varied photos per species that we identify as the main need.",
    161: "The most realistic use for now is as a guide's assistant and a learning tool. At a site like UWEC, where the animals are known, the app can show checked facts, safety notes and names in Ugandan languages and in the main languages of international visitors (text and audio), and recognition becomes better as site photos are added. This matches the smart-tourism idea that technology should support the visitor experience and not replace people [@gretzel,@tussyadiah].",
    173: "Tourism is one of Uganda's biggest earners of foreign money and it depends on wildlife, but visitors' experience of wildlife is limited by uneven guiding, few local-language explanations and poor internet access. This project set out to show how AI can help by building a free, offline phone tool that identifies animals from a visitor's photo and explains them.",
    174: "In Part 2 we built that tool, UgaWildVision, and measured it carefully. A 26-class YOLOv8n-cls classifier, trained on 3,636 images from the NESTLER Safari crops and internet sources, reaches 0.909 ± 0.007 top-1 accuracy on a standard test set. We exported it as a 5.9 MB ONNX model that gives the same answers as the original model and runs offline in a progressive web app on the phone. On our own phone photos from UWEC, however, it is right only 0.191 ± 0.030 of the time. Zebra and tiger work well, while chimpanzee, birds, lion and buffalo do not. Phone-style augmentation did not close the gap, and the model is confidently wrong on phone photos, so a confidence threshold cannot replace better data.",
    175: "Our main conclusion is that the limit is the variety of real photos for each animal, not the model size, the training method or the delivery platform. The app and the pipeline are ready to support tourism in Uganda. What they need is more varied photos, and tourism sites are well placed to provide them. With photos from the sites and checked local-language content, the tool can become a practical digital guide for the hundreds of thousands of Ugandan and foreign visitors to wildlife sites.",
}

LISTS = {
    24: ["build an image-classification dataset from the NESTLER Safari frames, internet image sources and our own UWEC photos, record the source of every image and correct wrongly labelled folders;",
         "train small YOLOv8 classification models that suit phones and choose one using the validation set only;",
         "measure accuracy on a standard test set and on our own phone photos taken as visitors at UWEC, which the model never trains on;",
         "test whether making training images look like phone photos reduces the gap between the two;",
         "deliver the model in an offline progressive web app for visitors, with facts, safety notes and local-language names, after checking that the app prepares photos exactly as in training."],
    6: ["Guiding skills are uneven. The World Bank says that roads and marketing alone will not turn visitors into jobs unless workers can give visitors a good experience, and that earlier training was not enough [@wb_jobs].",
        "Information in local languages is scarce. In 2018 the UWEC board chairperson told Parliament that guides often could not give animal names in local languages, and that many animal labels in parks were spelt wrongly [@dispatch]. Most UWEC visitors are Ugandans, many of them school groups, and a local name helps them remember an animal.",
        "Visitors speak many languages. In the latest survey with a breakdown by country (2019), Kenya alone supplied 26.4% of visitors, and the biggest groups of holiday visitors came from the USA, the United Kingdom, Kenya, Germany and Australia [@tsa2023]. Most information is only in English.",
        "Guides cannot be everywhere. Visitors who come alone, large school groups and visitors at busy times often see animals with nobody to explain them.",
        "Internet access is limited. Only 28.0% of Ugandans used the internet in January 2025, although mobile connections were equal to 76.2% of the population [@datareportal]. Network coverage in parks is also weak, so a tool that needs a server will often fail where it is needed."],
    26: ["Visitors: a free digital guide that is always available, works without a network and keeps photos on the phone.",
         "Guides and UWEC: the same facts, safety notes and local-language names for everyone, which supports guides instead of replacing them, including for large school groups.",
         "Domestic tourism: local-language information makes wildlife sites more interesting for the Ugandans who are most of UWEC's visitors.",
         "The tourism sector: a measured picture of how far current AI can go with the data available, and what data the sector needs to collect to make such tools reliable."],
    28: ["A documented 26-class dataset (cls_v1) that records the source, group and split of every image, with label corrections and near-duplicate removal.",
         "A comparison of accuracy on a standard test set (0.909) and on our own visitor-style phone photos (0.191), over three random seeds.",
         "An augmentation experiment (v2), planned before it was run, that did not improve the result.",
         "A working offline app for visitors, UgaWildVision, published online, whose model gives the same answers as the original model on 606 of 606 test images."],
    54: ["NESTLER crops are cut again from the original video frame with random extra space of U(0.15, 1.5) times the box size, so the animal is sometimes small in the picture. The extra space is reduced if the crop would include more than 20% of a bigger box of another class, and crops under 24 pixels are skipped.",
         "With probability 0.7 the image is shrunk so its shorter side is U(80, 500) pixels, enlarged again and saved as JPEG at quality U(30, 80), like a WhatsApp copy. With probability 0.3 a Gaussian blur is added.",
         "With probability 0.3 a fake chain-link fence is drawn over the image.",
         "With probability 0.5 the brightness, contrast and saturation are changed."],
    73: ["Translate the English name with the Sunflower translation model (POST /tasks/translate).",
         "Translate the result back to English, so that someone who does not know the language can see what it means.",
         "Check automatically: the name passes only if the back-translation names the same animal and the result is not just the English name again. Names that fail are kept for review but hidden in the app.",
         "For names that pass, create speech (POST /tasks/audio/speech) where Sunbird has a voice: Luganda, Runyankole, Ateso and Kiswahili. Rutooro has no voice yet, so it is text only. The audio files are saved inside the app and stored for offline use."],
    164: ["Our own photos are few: only 47 of the 5,187 internet and UWEC images. Most training images therefore do not look like visitor photos. The internet images are only used inside this project and cannot be published or shared again.",
          "Five classes have very few images (buffalo has one training image), and most of their photos are in the UWEC test set.",
          "The chimpanzee images come almost entirely from one NESTLER video. Many NESTLER bird crops are poor: 175 of 379 bird boxes lie at least 80% inside a bigger box of another class, and some show only hippo skin.",
          "The 'other' class has only animals. It has no people, empty enclosures or non-animal images, so the app cannot say 'no animal'.",
          "Folder names were not reliable. We corrected them by eye, and some mistakes may remain."],
    166: ["The UWEC test set is small (47 photos) and covers only 8 of the 26 classes, so the results for each class rest on 1 to 9 photos.",
          "We have looked at the same 47 photos many times (including when we thought of the fence idea), so we need a fresh set of unseen phone photos for the next fair measurement.",
          "We used three seeds per setting, so the standard deviations are only rough.",
          "The v1 and v2 comparison is affected by v2 having three times as many training steps per epoch."],
    168: ["The classifier names one animal per photo and works best when the animal fills the picture. There is no detector step for small or distant animals.",
          "Confidence is not reliable on phone photos, so the 'not sure' rule protects users only a little.",
          "When we wrote this, the app had only been tested in desktop Chrome and not yet on a range of real phones (camera, installing and offline use).",
          "The local-language names (text and audio) come from machine translation and speech synthesis, and native speakers must still check them.",
          "We have not tried the tool with real tourists, so we have not measured its effect on the visitor experience.",
          "Timings come from one laptop and one desktop browser, not from field devices.",
          "Machine translation of single animal names is not reliable when the name has no common local word. Our automatic back-translation check can pass a wrong name and reject a right one."],
    177: ["Collect about 30 or more original phone photos of each species at UWEC, from different angles and distances and through the fence, sent as original files and not WhatsApp copies. Train on part of them and keep a new unseen set for testing.",
          "Clean the NESTLER bird and chimpanzee crops, and add a detector step that crops small or distant animals before classification [@beery19,@gadot].",
          "Add non-animal images (people, empty enclosures) to the 'other' class.",
          "Test the app on several real phones (camera, installation, offline use and speed).",
          "Finish the local-language names and audio (Luganda, Runyankole, Rutooro, Ateso) with Sunbird AI [@sunbird] and have native speakers verify them before they are shown.",
          "Work with UWEC and the Uganda Wildlife Authority to try the app with visitors and guides and measure its effect on the visitor experience.",
          "Add more species and content for the main national parks, so one app serves visitors across Uganda's wildlife tourism circuit.",
          "Try stronger pretrained features such as BioCLIP [@bioclip] and better confidence calibration when data change [@guo,@ovadia].",
          "Build the planned Android app from the same ONNX model."],
}

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ short report
SHORT = {
    13: "The biggest known weakness of wildlife classifiers is that they do not work well in new conditions: models do very well where they were trained but badly at new locations [@beery18]. Training-set size, class imbalance and location are important factors [@schneider,@norman], and benchmarks such as WILDS show the same gap in many areas [@wilds]. When local data are scarce, projects often borrow public images. Flickr and iNaturalist photos can work well, especially with some images from the target place added [@shepley], and large collections such as iNat2021 [@inat21] and the BioCLIP model [@bioclip] show how far this can go. Classifying cropped animals instead of whole images also helps [@gadot].",
    15: None,
    17: "On the phone we need small networks such as MobileNetV3 [@mobilenet] and the YOLOv8 classifiers [@yolov8], and speed differs a lot between phone chips [@aibench]. A model exported to ONNX can run in a browser with ONNX Runtime Web [@ort], and progressive web apps install from a link and work offline [@pwa]. We train with data augmentation [@shorten,@randaug] and the AdamW optimiser [@adamw]. Networks are often over-confident, especially when the data change [@guo,@ovadia], so the app uses the softmax confidence only as a simple 'not sure' signal [@hendrycks]. We also avoid data leakage between training and test sets by splitting by session [@kapoor].",
    19: None,
    36: "We used three sources of images, and all three help the model. The first is the NESTLER Safari video dataset, part of the NESTLER Wild Animal Recognition Video Dataset on Zenodo [@nestler,@z_data]: 1,220 video frames and 3,909 boxes from Kibale, Queen Elizabeth and Bwindi National Parks. We cut each box out as a crop with 15% extra space and skipped boxes shorter than 32 pixels. The second source is 5,187 internet images (images.cv, iNaturalist and other websites) in folders by animal, and the third is our own 47 phone photos taken at UWEC in Entebbe. A script recorded the size, camera data, hash, folder, corrected class and source of every image, and the file image_sources.csv lists where each internet image came from. Some folders were wrongly labelled (for example zebras in the buffalo folder), so we fixed them after looking at the pictures and removed exact duplicates. {T:sources} lists the sources.",
    38: "UWEC is in Entebbe, so our own photos and the UWEC dataset come from the same place. The NESTLER and internet images are used for training, validation and testing, and our own 47 photos are the final real-world test. We kept them out of training on purpose so that at least one test is fully independent. The internet images are only used inside this project and are not shared again; no figure in the report shows one.",
    43: "The dataset has 26 classes: 20 regular animal classes, five classes with very few images that we kept at the team's request and marked in the app (lion, buffalo, cheetah, kudu and hyena, with 1 to 14 training images each), and an 'other' class so that the app can say 'not one of my species'.",
    45: "We split the data by recording session or photo group, never by single images, because nearly identical images on both sides of a split make the test score look better than it is [@kapoor]. {T:splitrules} shows the rules.",
    53: "In a second experiment (v2) we asked whether making the training images look more like phone photos would reduce the gap to our UWEC photos. We added two changed copies of each training image (wider or smaller views, lower quality and blur, a fake fence and colour changes), with the same probabilities for every class. We fixed the recipe before seeing any v2 result, and the validation and test images were the same as in v1.",
    55: None,
    57: "We choose the model for the app using the best validation top-1 accuracy, never the test or UWEC accuracy. The validation set (553 images) is used to pick the best epoch (one full pass through the training images) and the best run, and to tell training when to stop. We report two test sets: the test set (559 images from the same sources as the training data) and uwec_test (our 47 phone photos). The metrics are:",
    61: "Top-1 is the share of photos named correctly, top-5 is the share where the true class is among the five most likely answers, and macro accuracy is the average of the accuracy of each class. We give results over seeds as mean ± standard deviation.",
    63: "We export the model to ONNX [@ort]. A check script compared the app's resize and crop with training on all 606 validation and test images, and the ONNX model gives the same answer as PyTorch on every one of them.",
    65: "UgaWildVision is a progressive web app ({F:pwa}). The visitor takes or chooses a photo, and the app prepares it exactly as in training, runs the model on the phone and shows the top answer with its confidence. Below 0.7 it says it is not sure and asks for a closer photo. All files are saved on the phone, so the app works offline and photos never leave the phone.",
    67: "Around the classifier the app is a pocket field guide ({F:appscreens}): a home page with a QR code, a searchable list of animals, and a page for each animal with a photo, its name in the chosen language with a Listen button, its conservation status, a safety note and short facts. Visitors can also save sightings. The guide photos are openly licensed Wikimedia Commons pictures, credited in the app.",
    69: "The published site also has an install page with a QR code and short instructions, which can be printed and put up at a site entrance.",
    76: "International visitors need their own languages. To avoid choosing them just by preference, we used a fixed rule on the official table of visitors by country of residence [@tsa2023]. A language is included if visitors from countries where it is a main language make up at least 2% of all visitors or of leisure visitors ({T:langsel}). Kiswahili, French, German and Dutch pass. Italian, Spanish, Arabic and Russian do not.",
    79: "The Kiswahili names come from Sunbird AI. The French, German and Dutch names are common names checked against Wikipedia [@wikipedia] and are spoken by the phone's own voice [@webspeech].",
    93: "All runs finished without errors and stopped early. Validation top-1 accuracy rose above 0.8 within a few epochs and stayed near 0.90 ({F:trainval}). The best small model, v1_n_seed2 (validation top-1 0.910), was chosen for the app.",
    100: "On our UWEC photos, only zebra and tiger worked well ({T:uwecclass}). Chimpanzee, bird and buffalo were never correct in any run. So the model does well in some cases and needs more varied photos in others.",
    117: "Of the 125 Sunbird AI names (25 species in 5 languages), 44 passed the automatic check and are shown in the app, 39 of them with audio ({F:langcheck}). Common animals with a well-known local word, such as lion (Luganda Empologoma, Runyankole Entare) or bird (Ekinyonyi), were translated well. Animals with compound or foreign names, such as Uganda kob or kudu, were not.",
    120: None,
    124: "{F:testuwec} shows the two test sets side by side for each run. All runs score 0.90 to 0.92 on the test set and 0.15 to 0.23 on the UWEC photos, so the gap comes from the data and not from one unlucky run.",
    128: "{F:sources} shows where the images come from: mostly NESTLER Safari crops and internet images, while the UWEC test photos are a kind of image the model never saw in training. The classes with very few images have more test photos than training images (buffalo: 1 training image, 9 test photos), so we could not expect them to work.",
    135: "{F:conf} compares the confidence of correct and wrong answers. On the test set wrong answers are rare and less confident, so the 0.7 threshold separates them well. On UWEC photos 37 of 47 answers are wrong with an average confidence of 0.80, so most pass the threshold.",
    139: "{F:v1v2} summarises the v2 experiment. The test score goes up a little, but the UWEC scores overlap within their spread, and chimpanzee, bird and buffalo stay at zero.",
    149: "The model does well on animals whose training images are many different photos, such as zebra and tiger. It does badly where the images look alike: the chimpanzee images come almost entirely from one video, and the NESTLER birds are small egrets, unlike the owls and parrots at UWEC. Phone-style augmentation changes how an image looks but cannot add this missing variety, which is why v2 did not help [@shepley].",
}

